        ":question/id": "91496801-bb35-528a-8bb8-dd007b8e0c83",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-65549",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/25/25cdbb7ea69245986f5fc3df65490aa6ca72d0adcf319a768c5243d7b692e3d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-2D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=5-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=5-3=2.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"
      },
