        ":question/id": "d92d6b12-34ec-5e74-a8fa-e75755bbf55a",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-79188",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/ae/ae6a514e0d9cde3db3c3b1dfa94e3804b5469dd5fb99b40a94684e88b573ec38.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$"
