        ":question/problem": "![](images/60/6003a85e0038b7e6562381cd338a7597877d1152adbeb476b7141eee74ba06ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"
