        ":question/problem": "![](images/14/148ef6b6d54262df159703437a6003e8f884052b98e95eb61536890a987a07f1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{9}{4}=-1.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"
