        ":question/problem": "![](images/f8/f86984919b15283134e1b3a9479ff8c79ec8dfacc84975e82f88704b7b26dd3a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B-2D?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=4.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=4-2\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4})=4+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"
